IP addresses (5 points)

Implement an IP address datatype to the WHILE language scanner and parser. Add a way to declare IP addresses with the address keyword. An IP address can either be in the IPv4 or 
the IPv6 form. IP expressions can be constructed with the @IPv4 or @IPv6 methods, like in the example below. Both of these methods take their argument inside parentheses.

IPv4 addresses consist of four numbers ("octets") separated by periods. Each octet is a number from 0 to 255, and they do not contain leading zeros.

IPv6 addresses consist of eight groups of four hexadecimal digits (digits from 0-9 and letters from A-F), separated by colons. The letter "digits" may be written in uppercase or 
lowercase, and you may mix them inside the address. (Note: in the official standard, there are also abbreviation rules for IPv6 addresses, but this task doesn't require them.)

IP expressions are not complex expressions. Both (...)@IPv4 and (...)@IPv6 methods should be scanned as a single token!

Example for a working program:

program ip_addresses
    address ipaddress1;
    address ipaddress2;
begin
    ipaddress1 := (192.168.1.1)@IPv4;
    ipaddress2 := (2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:ff00:0042:8329)@IPv6;
    
    write(ipaddress1.version);
    write(ipaddress1.isLoopback);
    write(ipaddress1.isPrivate);
    
    if ipaddress2.isPrivate then
        write(ipaddress2);
    else
        if ipaddress2.isLoopback then
            write(ipaddress2);
        endif
    endif
end

IP addresses have three "getter" expressions. We can get the version of the address with .version. .isLoopback states whether the address points inside the network itself. 
isPrivate states if the address is in the private address range. Identifiers with one of these "getter" expressions should yield a more complex expression.
Try-catch (5 points)

Implement very basic exception handling for the WHILE language with try-catch statements. First, add three basic exception types called badarith, divbyzero and badtype to the 
scanner. These should all be keywords, returning separate tokens. A try expressions consists of a try keyword, with statements after it and a list of catch clauses. Each catch 
clause begins with the catch keyword, followed by one of the three exception types, then by an arrow (->), and then the statements to be executed in case the exception is caught. 
There can be multiple catch blocks, but always at least one. The try statement always ends in an endtry, with no semicolon needed.

Example for a correct program:

program trycatch
    integer a;
    integer b;
begin
    try
        a := 10;
        b := a div 0;
        a := b div a;
    catch badarith ->
        write(0);
        skip;
    catch badtype ->
        skip;
        write(0);
    catch divbyzero ->
        a := 0;
        b := 0;
        write(a + b);
    endtry
    skip;
end

Note: make sure try statements can be used inside each other!

These two tasks can earn you a bonus point. I only recommend you to do them if you've completed everything else. I will not give partial points for partial solutions to these
bonus tasks.

Bonus for 0.15 points: Implement a "throw" statement. These statements begin with the keyword throw, and then one of the three exception types is given.

Example:

program trycatch
    integer a;
    integer b;
begin
    try
        if b = 0 then
            throw badarith;
        else
            write(a div b);
        endif
    catch badarith ->
        write(0);
    endtry
end

Bonus for 0.85 points: Implement a "finally" clause. "finally" is an optional clause, that has to be put after the catch clauses. It starts with the finally keyword and an arrow 
(->), which is then followed by statements. There can at most one finally clause.

Example:

program trycatch
    integer a;
begin
   try
       a := a div (a - a);
       write(123);
   catch divbyzero ->
       write(234);
   finally ->
       write(345);
   endtry
end

Intervals (5 points)

Implement intervals and some operations on them. You can declare an interval with the interval keyword. Interval literals start with the lowercase letters in, followed by two 
numbers inside square brackets, with two periods in between. (Intervals to infinity are not supported.) Interval literals should be scanned as a whole token. You don't need to 
prevent leading zeros in the two numbers.

program intervals
    interval a;
    interval b;
    interval c;
    interval d;
begin
    a := in[1..3];
    b := in[3..10];
    c := in[10..12];
    d := in[2..5];
    
    write(a ^ b | c & d);
end

Implement three operations on the interval types: union (notated by |), intersection (notated by &), and symmetric difference (notated by ^). These operations join two expressions 
together, making a more complex expression. All interval operations are right associative. Interval operations have higher priority than the comparison operations, but lower 
priority that addition or subtraction. Among these new operations, symmetric difference (^) has the highest precedence, intersection (&) is in the middle and union (|) has the 
lowest.
